answer=Storyboard Programming is a synthesis framework “designed to help programmers write imperative low-level data-structure manipulations.” It aims to bridge the gap between the “boxes-and-arrows” diagrams programmers use to think about algorithms and the low-level code that implements them.

According to the documents, it synthesizes implementations from:
- “a set of partial input-output examples,” and
- “a description of the high-level structure of the desired solution.”

It then “is able to synthesize low-level imperative implementations in a matter of minutes.”

The documents also say the framework is based on combining “constraint-based synthesis and abstract-interpretation” and that the tool uses “abstract and concrete input-output examples as well as implementation skeletons.”

So, in short: storyboard programming lets users specify the intended transformation with examples, diagrams, and skeletons, and the synthesizer turns that into pointer-manipulating code.
